is is a reality in economies  like ours that are heavily driven by the  perception of their own State  and that is that people fearing a  recession starting to spend less can  precipitate one but also people feeling  as though the situation has been  resolved and starting to spend more and  demand more in and of itself can help to  get us out of a recession so yeah people  feeling like the recession is over is a  big part of the recession being over and  it can seem recursive or endlessly  regressive this is arguably not good  about our economic system I think it's  important to understand that so much of  how we are doing as an economy is about  how people believe that we are doing but  for now this is our system and consumer  sentiment is part of getting out of a  depression because when people believe  we are out or soon to be out they start  acting as though we are out which gets  us out because people start spending and  demanding goods and services so let's  think back to 2008 ok the Great  Recession of 2008 two things happened to  stop that recession and to get us out of  it number one the underlying issue which  was the fear of huge financial  institutions being insolvent as a result  of the mortgage crisis and some other  reasons that was resolved sort of in the  sense that we had some new regulations  we had the belief that we've put in  place new lending guidelines so the  mortgage crisis can't happen again we've  cleaned up some corruption supposedly  even though nobody went to jail but the  point is there was the belief that the  underlying cause had been resolved that  was a big part of it in addition though  we had a lot of economic policy response  under the Obama administration that got  us out that included that huge stimulus  package that Republicans were furious  about that Barack Obama did it included  new government spending it included some  tax cuts so that was 2008 the difference  now with the current recession or what  some are saying is an inevitable  depression that's coming related to  coronavirus is that it is not a  financial institution recession in  principle what we have here is a corona  virus pandemic  which decimated demand for stuff because  of the pandemic because of the virus  demand for travel went down 90 95  percent in some cases demand for office  space collapsed demand for restaurants  collapsed all of that stuff so the  resolution to this one is not about  government spending money or sending  people cash government needs to do that  as a stopgap so that people don't starve  or lose their homes or lose their cars  or lose their businesses that they hope  to go back to but the underlying issue  here to be solved is a medical issue we  need treatment we need a vaccine so for  now government's role is a little bit  different in this recession than it was  in 2008 government needs to provide  support for people to keep them and  businesses afloat in a sort of waiting  period it's a bridge in a sense once the  underlying issue the virus is resolved  demand in principle should start to  recover now it's only going to recover  in proportion to people's ability to  demand things now when we say demand  we're talking about it economically I  can demand a private jet in the sense  that it sounds cool but when I talk  about economic demand it means that  there is actually the money with which  to demand these things in an economic  system so as people feel safe going to  restaurants they will go to restaurants  and the restaurants will make money  they'll pay employees they'll buy stuff  from food supply companies the employees  will go out and spend money at other  local businesses the food supply  companies seeing more demand from  restaurants will start ordering more  food from producers and the economic  activity will resume traveling will you  know then increase but increased air  travel will mean that people are going  to be places they don't live which means  they will demand hotel stays and on and  on and on now a reasonable question  would be and one that we should be  asking is there a way to prevent these  recessions in the first place now  preventing them altogether is tough and  there's a difference between a medically  based recession like coronavirus and  something like we saw in 2008 the  financial crisis  no doubt that we could organize  economies in a way that would soften  these recessions and make them less  severe there are some realities about  our economic system um there are booms  and busts that's how capitalism works  and we have a capitalism in the United  States that is not nearly regulated  enough there is no incentive in systems  like ours to be proactive proactive  means you're spending money you might  not need to spend and you're reducing  your profits in the immediate that we  being proactive is not incentivized by  the system that we have we are reactive  so when the boom turns to a bust it's  really big and a ton of people suffer  greatly in the last three months we  learned about this many systems that  feel strong low unemployment high stock  market high consumer confidence looked  like a very strong infrastructure it was  really quite fragile in about a two-week  period it imploded if all along we had a  more solid infrastructure we would  suffer not nearly as much during these  downturns so for example if everybody  had healthcare not connected to their  employer losing a job during a pandemic  would be less of a crisis it would still  be a crisis but it would be less of a  crisis if health insurance was not tied  to employment another example if we had  universal basic income or something like  it  jobs guarantee whatever flavor you like  the loss of jobs or reductions in wages  would be less problematic  it wouldn't be irrelevant but it would  be less urgently problematic and the  government would not be in as much of a  position of acutely having to figure out  we got to start sending out checks  direct deposits debit prepaid debit  cards all this different stuff it would  be less of a crisis so the the lesson  that hopefully we would learn here is we  can't prevent a future pandemic from a  novel virus could happen again it likely  will happen again we can't prevent any  up-and-down in the economy even if we  ensure you know putting it in different  insurance policies there are business  cycles and there are going to be ups and  down  but what Social Democrats like me want  is more investment from the fruits of  when things are going well to put in  place social insurance policies that  will raise the standard of living for  everybody get more people off of that  borderline paycheck-to-paycheck  situation where if you don't work for  two weeks  you now are unable to meet your basic  expenses let's make the system less  fragile for when things like this happen  in the future  it's no secret on the show that I am  very critical of the big banks and how  they screw over customers in so many  ways and if you're like me you're  probably sick of the fees and the  hassles that come with a checking  account at a big traditional bank by now  in that case check out the online bank  current they're one of our sponsors if  you're watching on YouTube this here is  my actual debit card for my current  checking account which I'm holding up  with current you've got no minimum  balance no overdraft fees up to $100 no  ATM fees you can use over fifty five  thousand ATMs nationwide unlike with  other banks you receive your direct  deposit instantly meaning you'll get  paid by your employer up to two days  sooner you can deposit checks instantly  using your camera phone 24/7 member  support and unlike with other banks  current instantly refunds that hold on  your card when you pump gas you can  pause your debit card instantly which is  really useful there are just so many  different advantages to ditching the big  banks and instead use a bank that puts  their members first it takes two minu